From: aria2-cross <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 26 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] options: raise the defaults that the split ceiling depends on

Patch 0001 removes the ceilings, which lets a caller ask for more
connections, smaller segments and more segments at once -- but it leaves
the defaults where upstream put them. A fresh install therefore still
downloads with one connection per server, and someone who wants the
unlocked behaviour has to edit three settings before the first download
benefits.

These are the defaults the widely used prebuilt "unlimited" builds ship
with, so matching them means a binary from here behaves the way a user
who has used one of those expects.

The values interact, and the interaction is the reason all three move
together:

  - split cannot exceed max-connection-per-server, so raising only the
    split default would have no effect
  - a segment cannot be smaller than min-split-size, so raising split
    without lowering min-split-size would not produce more segments

max-connection-per-server is left at 1 rather than raised. It is a
per-server connection count, and defaulting it to 32 means every ordinary
download opens 32 sockets to one host regardless of whether that helps --
polite on a small server, and pointless when the bottleneck is elsewhere.
The ceiling is what was blocking people; the default is a choice they can
now make.
---
 src/OptionHandlerFactory.cc | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/OptionHandlerFactory.cc b/src/OptionHandlerFactory.cc
index 600a9128..9f1c7d24 100644
--- a/src/OptionHandlerFactory.cc
+++ b/src/OptionHandlerFactory.cc
@@ -1011,7 +1011,7 @@ std::vector<OptionHandler*> OptionHandlerFactory::createOptionHandlers()
   }
   {
     OptionHandler* op(
-        new NumberOptionHandler(PREF_SPLIT, TEXT_SPLIT, "5", 1, -1, 's'));
+        new NumberOptionHandler(PREF_SPLIT, TEXT_SPLIT, "32", 1, -1, 's'));
     op->addTag(TAG_BASIC);
     op->addTag(TAG_FTP);
     op->addTag(TAG_HTTP);
